      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"hamsun-victoria-6e2952ea15b26d6d\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"The old woman’s “jeg elsker dig til døden” is principally an unequivocal avowal that her love persists despite her husband’s disfigurement. “Til døden” gives the avowal a weak vow-like implication of lifelong devotion, but the clause does not clearly undertake a new obligation. Her stammering, exclamation, and hand-kissing mark intense delivery without independently making the highlighted clause an expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The phrase “til døden” can weakly renew marital commitment as well as describe the extremity and endurance of her love; hence limited P support. The affective delivery creates some superficial E pressure, but T plus contextual intensity adequately captures it.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"quoted_or_revoiced\",\"description\":\"Direct speech attributed to the old wife inside the inset love story narrated by the monk Vendt.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The husband has deliberately disfigured himself and asks whether his wife still loves him. Her answer reassures him by affirming continued attraction and love unto death. It culminates an idealized account of reciprocal marital constancy in which each spouse responds to the other’s disfigurement or disability with sacrificial solidarity. The framing and heightened delivery intensify the avowal but do not displace its central truth-conditional force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“din røst gjør mig endnu den dag idag het i hjærtet og jeg elsker dig til døden”\",\"supports\":\"The reference to her continuing bodily-emotional response and the explicit present-tense love clause strongly support a sincere-seeming avowal of an enduring state (T); “til døden” provides limited vow-like P support.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The husband disfigures his face with acid and asks, “du elsker mig vel ikke mere?”\",\"supports\":\"Her utterance directly answers a challenge to the continued existence of her love, making reassurance through assertion or avowal its primary force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“O, du min brudgom, min elskede! stammer den gamle kvinde og kysser hans hænder.”\",\"supports\":\"The stammering, vocatives, and hand-kissing establish intense affective delivery, but they do not by themselves show that the highlighted love clause has independent expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The inset narrative recounts decades of mutual love and reciprocal self-disfigurement or sacrifice between the spouses.\",\"supports\":\"The sustained marital history supports the interpretation of the clause as an avowal of constancy, with a secondary implication of continuing relational devotion.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal, while a small P score registers the vow-like resonance of loving “unto death.” Delivery and narrative intensity can remain contextual without requiring E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
